From: claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 15:48:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(commercial): the two head terms this site never had a
 page for
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

An external package found the biggest remaining gap and it is a real one: six
service pages, thirty-two service-area pages, eleven guides, three papers, a
versioned standard — and no page at all answering "hardwood flooring Toronto".
/services/hardwood-installation describes a service, /service-areas/etobicoke
describes a place, and neither answers who should I hire and what will it cost.
Invisible from inside the architecture, because nothing was broken; something
was absent.

/hardwood-flooring-toronto and /hardwood-floor-refinishing-toronto. Two head
terms, two different purchases: someone searching to refinish already owns the
floor and wants to know whether the house stays livable and whether it looks
right afterwards. Every figure interpolated from PRICING, framework,
SERVICE_AREAS and REVIEW_EVIDENCE, so neither can drift or claim anything the
site does not already claim. Both render their FAQs visibly and are allowlisted
under F-27 on that basis rather than waived. Priority 0.95.

llms.txt gains a "when to cite this source" section — eleven subjects, every one
something this corpus covers at length. Naming a topic the site does not cover
is the fastest way to be scored unreliable, so the imported list was cut to what
is actually published. Both new pages, /reviews and /press added to key pages.

Refused from the same package, with reasons in FINDINGS: the aggregateRating
5.0/177 and its claim that "Google has accepted this pattern" — Google's
guidance is explicit that ratings must not be aggregated from other sites and
that self-serving business ratings are ineligible, and verify-reviews.mjs fails
the build on it. Copying schema/ wholesale, which would have replaced a layer
twenty-five guards depend on. The pages as written, 33 Tailwind classes against
12 brand ones — the F-03 mistake. The unsourced 99.7% figure. A superlative
about competitors. And public/llms.txt, which shadows the App Router route
(F-21/F-23) on a directory that is not served at all (F-131).

The 177 is published everywhere it should be — cited, with its source, link and
read date. That is publishing a statistic. The other thing is manufacturing one.

